<h2>Why a separate statute exists</h2>
<p>The maintenance jurisdiction of criminal courts has always covered parents, but Parliament concluded in 2007 that elderly applicants needed something faster, cheaper and less adversarial than even a summary criminal proceeding. The result is an administrative adjudication model: Maintenance Tribunals constituted district-wise and presided over by officers of the executive, applications that can be filed by the parent personally, by an authorised person or organisation, or even taken up by the Tribunal on its own motion, and conciliation built into the process before adjudication.</p>

<h2>The proceeding, step by step</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Application.</strong> Filed before the Tribunal for the district where the applicant or the children reside. No court fee is payable. The application states the applicant&rsquo;s needs, means and the children&rsquo;s capacity.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Interim allowance.</strong> The Tribunal may order interim maintenance during the pendency of the proceeding — important, because the applicant is by definition a person unable to maintain themselves.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Conciliation.</strong> The Tribunal may refer the matter to a conciliation officer; a settlement reached is recorded and carries the force of the Tribunal&rsquo;s order. Many family maintenance disputes genuinely end here.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Inquiry and order.</strong> Failing settlement, a summary inquiry follows, with the statute setting a ninety-day norm for disposal (extendable once for recorded reasons). The order directs a monthly allowance, within the prescribed ceiling, payable from the date of the order or application as the Tribunal directs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Enforcement.</strong> Defaults are recovered in the manner of fines, and the Act provides imprisonment for continued non-compliance — a sanction administrative in origin but criminal in bite. Abandonment of a senior citizen is separately punishable under the Act.</div>
</div>

<h2>Choosing between the 2007 Act and Section 144 BNSS</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Senior Citizens Act, 2007</th><th>Section 144 BNSS</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Forum</td><td>Maintenance Tribunal (executive officer)</td><td>Judicial Magistrate / Family Court</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>State-prescribed, up to Rs 10,000/month</td><td>No statutory ceiling; turns on means</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Representation</td><td>Legal practitioners excluded; maintenance officer may assist</td><td>Advocates appear as usual</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Timeline</td><td>Ninety-day norm</td><td>No fixed norm; interim relief available</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Extra powers</td><td>Section 23 avoidance of conditional transfers; protection of life and property; eviction directions in aid of the senior citizen&rsquo;s rights as recognised by the rules and case law</td><td>Enforcement by warrant and, for arrears, the one-year application rule</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The Section 23 jurisdiction deserves emphasis, because it has no counterpart anywhere else in maintenance law. Elderly parents are routinely persuaded to gift the family home to a child on an assurance of care; when the care stops, Section 23 allows the Tribunal to unwind the gift itself, restoring the parent&rsquo;s ownership rather than merely ordering a monthly sum. The condition of maintenance need not always be written into the deed — tribunals and courts examine the surrounding circumstances — but a recital in the document makes the case far stronger, and families structuring such transfers should take advice before, not after, signing.</p>

<h2>Delhi practice</h2>
<p>In Delhi, the Tribunals function at the district level under the Delhi rules framed under the Act, with appellate authority vested in the Divisional Commissioner&rsquo;s administration, and a dedicated framework for summary eviction of abusive occupants from a senior citizen&rsquo;s property has developed through the rules and the writ jurisprudence of the High Court. The forum is genuinely accessible: applications are routinely filed and decided without counsel, though families facing contested property questions under Section 23 should have the papers settled professionally even if the hearing itself proceeds without advocates.</p>
<div class="note">This article describes the statutory scheme in general terms. Ceilings, rules and appellate designations are State-specific and change; the current Delhi rules should be checked before filing. Nothing here is advice in any matter.</div>
